src('colley_yale','https://yalebooks.yale.edu/book/9780300152807/britons/','Publisher description connects nation formation to war, religion, trade and empire. The linked revised edition is 2009 with a new preface, not the original 1992 event.')
src('deloria_playing_yale','https://yalebooks.yale.edu/book/9780300153606/playing-indian/','Philip J. Deloria examines how non-Native appropriation of Indianness shaped American national identities alongside conquest, dispossession and Native responses. Publisher identifies the 1998 work separately from the 2022 paperback.')
src('deloria_unexpected_kansas','https://kansaspress.ku.edu/9780700614592/','Publisher identifies Philip J. Deloria and the 2004 book, which challenges expectations that Native people belong outside modernity through cases involving cars, opera, film and other activities. This is a conceptual intervention through specific histories, not merely an additional topical study.')
src('mitchell_rule_uc','https://www.ucpress.edu/books/rule-of-experts/paper','Mitchell 2002 investigates the production of techno-politics, property and the national economy through Egyptian histories. It questions coherent capital and self-understandings of technoscience; not a generic claim that experts control everything.')
src('mitchell_colonising_primary','https://publishing.cdlib.org/ucpressebooks/public/book/colonising-egypt.html','The June 1991 paperback preface describes colonial ordering, representation and disciplinary power. Mitchell explicitly uses and qualifies Foucault: disciplinary arrangements can fail or create spaces of resistance. The original work is 1988, the new preface 1991.','Author preface read, especially pages ix–xiii and closing argument summary; remaining book unread')
src('arendt_bard','https://hac.bard.edu/about/hannaharendt/','Arendt Center bibliography distinguishes Origins 1951 from enlarged 1958 and later editions including 1973. The corpus 1973 year must not become an original-publication date.')
src('arendt_loc','https://www.loc.gov/collections/hannah-arendt-papers/articles-and-essays/totalitarianism-the-inversion-of-politics/','Collection essay and quoted Arendt reflections distinguish retrospectively identified origins/elements from straightforward causal necessity. Her totalitarianism analysis connects antisemitism and imperialism without claiming these automatically caused Nazism or Stalinism.','Institutional archival essay and its selected Arendt quotations read; book unread')
src('arendt_chicago','https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/H/bo29137972.html','Publisher identifies Human Condition as first published in 1958, with later enlarged editions; the corpus 1998 date is not the original.')
src('arendt_canovan','https://press.uchicago.edu/dam/ucp/books/pdf/course_intro/978-0-226-58660-1-introduction.pdf','Canovan introduction identifies distinctions between labour, work and action, power/violence/strength, and earth/world. This named reading supports a conceptual-coverage proposal rather than claiming direct primary-book reading.','Publisher introduction opening extract checked; attributed to Margaret Canovan')
src('mosse_wisconsin_press','https://uwpress.wisc.edu/Books/T/The-Nationalization-of-the-Masses','Publisher indexed description identifies the 1975 original and 2023 edition. It describes political symbolism, secular religion, political liturgy and mass mobilization across Germany and comparative settings.','Publisher search extract checked; direct open restricted; book unread')
src('mosse_wisconsin_memorial','https://history.wisc.edu/people-main/in-memoriam/','Faculty memorial identifies Mosse’s work on political symbolism (1975), war mourning/monuments (1990), nationalism/sexuality (1985) and modern masculinity (1996), establishing the cross-field programme and bibliographic dates.','Institutional memorial extract checked; book arguments unread')
src('corbin_primary_interview','https://www.persee.fr/doc/polix_0295-2319_1993_num_6_21_2024?pageId=T1_8','In the 1993 interview page 8, Corbin discusses criticism of reified mentalities, acknowledges Foucault’s intervention, identifies Febvre’s 1941 programme on perception and sensibilities, and explains his interest in social representations and difficulty recovering dynamic traces.','Primary interview page 8 read in French; English paraphrase; not full interview')
